        <div id="tutorial-header"></div>
        
        <div class="content lesson-content" style="font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--text-secondary);">

            <p>While often used interchangeably, bandwidth and throughput are fundamentally different concepts in networking.</p>
            
            <h3>Bandwidth</h3>
            <p><strong>Bandwidth</strong> is the theoretical or configured maximum capacity of a network link. It is the size of the "pipe." If you purchase a 1 Gbps internet connection, your bandwidth is 1 Gigabit per second.</p>
            
            <h3>Throughput</h3>
            <p><strong>Throughput</strong> is the actual rate of useful data transferred over a period of time. A 1 Gbps link does <em>not</em> automatically mean you get 1 Gbps application download performance. Throughput is almost always lower than bandwidth.</p>
            
            <h3>Goodput</h3>
            <p><strong>Goodput</strong> is the actual application-level payload delivered, excluding all protocol overhead (like Ethernet, IP, and <a href="tcp-ip-model-explained.html">TCP</a> headers) and any packet retransmissions.</p>
            
            <h3>Factors Influencing Throughput</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Protocol Overhead:</strong> IP and TCP headers consume bytes that aren't application data. <a href="mtu-and-mss-explained.html">VPN overhead</a> consumes even more.</li>
                <li><strong>Latency and TCP Behavior:</strong> High <a href="network-latency-troubleshooting.html">latency</a> can limit TCP throughput due to window sizing mechanics.</li>
                <li><strong>Packet Loss:</strong> Dropped packets require retransmissions, severely reducing goodput.</li>
                <li><strong>Hardware Limits:</strong> Server CPU limits, slow storage drives, or congested Wi-Fi environments.</li>
            </ul>
            
            <h3>Bits vs. Bytes</h3>
            <p>Network speeds are measured in <strong>bits</strong> (lowercase 'b'), while file sizes are measured in <strong>Bytes</strong> (uppercase 'B'). Because <strong>8 bits = 1 Byte</strong>, a theoretical 100 Mbps connection maxes out at 12.5 MB/s (100 / 8 = 12.5) <em>before</em> any overhead or limitations are applied.</p>
        
        </div>
        
